dev/plans/runs/codex/agent-seat-hardening/ASH-B-fix-1-20260727T182705Z.log:3309:    "FIX-1 / FINDING 2 — THE COMMENT/CODE RECONCILIATION THE ORCHESTRATOR REQUIRED. Finding 2 was at root a DRIFT defect: the header claimed `>|` coverage the code did not implement. So every form the comment claims is now asserted by an arm rather than believed, and the comment was rewritten to point at those arms by number. Result of auditing the whole matcher: `>` `>>` `1>` `2>>` in both spaced and glued shape all worked as documented (now pinned by arms 6, 10, 56a-56d). TWO FURTHER MISMATCHES WERE FOUND, and BOTH were resolved toward the CODE, because in both cases the code was right and the comment was merely silent or wrong. (a) `&>` and `&>>` WORK — the `&` split leaves `>FILE`/`>>FILE`, which the glued branch catches — but the comment never claimed them. Resolved by DOCUMENTING them and adding arms 56e/56f. (b) `>>|` and `&>|`: I first wrote the comment to say these are 'not covered'. That was itself wrong, because the `>|`->`>` fold makes them deny incidentally. Rather than leave a fresh mismatch, I measured them — `bash -n -c 'printf x >>| /dev/null'` and the `&>|` equivalent are both SYNTAX ERRORS, while `>|` and `2>|` parse fine — and the comment now records exactly that: no write can occur through either form, so nothing needs guarding, and the incidental deny is a harmless over-block on a command that could never run. No arm claims `>>|`/`&>|` coverage.",
dev/plans/runs/codex/agent-seat-hardening/ASH-B-fix-1-20260727T182705Z.log:3311:    "DESIGN FINDING THE STEWARD ASKED FOR — CAN THE HOOK DETERMINE WHICH AGENT/SEAT IS INVOKING IT? The honest answer is QUALIFIED YES-AND-NO, and the 'no' half is the important one. YES for a SPAWNED subagent: the PreToolUse payload carries `agent_type` (e.g. \"orchestrator\") alongside `agent_id`, so a spawned orchestrator/steward is positively identifiable with no cooperation from the agent and no configuration. NO for a MAIN-THREAD session: per the Claude Code v2.1.220 binary's own field documentation, `agent_id` is \"Absent for the main thread, even in --agent sessions\", and `agent_type` is present on the main thread ONLY for a session started with `--agent`. A `/orchestrate` or `/steward` session is an ORDINARY session running a slash command — no `--agent` flag — so its top-level thread carries NEITHER field. From the payload alone such a session is byte-for-byte indistinguishable from any other session in this repo, including a human's ad-hoc one. There is no way to close that gap from inside a hook: the information simply is not sent. CONSEQUENCE FOR THE SEAT MODEL: the hook mechanically guards the spawned case and can only OPT-IN guard the main-thread case, via the $FATHOMDB_SEAT environment variable set at session LAUNCH. Since dev/design/orchestration.md § 1.1 establishes that 'main thread' is role-indexed and that a /steward session's top-level thread IS the Steward, the usual Steward seat is exactly the unguarded case unless the launcher sets the env var. That is a real hole in the guard, not a rounding error, and it is stated in the hook header rather than papered over.",
dev/plans/runs/codex/agent-seat-hardening/ASH-B-fix-1-20260727T182705Z.log:3315:    "Created .claude/hooks/seat-path-guard.sh — a PreToolUse hook implementing the dev/design/orchestration.md § 1.2 write-path boundary. Guarded seats: orchestrator, steward (matched case-insensitively, whitespace-trimmed). Denied: any path with a `src`, `engine`, or `tests` SEGMENT, plus test-source basenames (test_*.{py,sh,rs,ts,js}, *_test.{py,sh,rs,go,ts,js}, *.test.{ts,tsx,js,mjs}, *.spec.{ts,tsx,js,mjs}, conftest.py). Matching is segment-based on the NORMALIZED target (`.`/`..` resolved, surrounding quotes stripped), so it is identical for absolute paths, repo-relative paths, and paths inside ANY worktree — a guarded seat must not write source anywhere, not only under $CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR.",
